data: {"choices":[{"delta":{"reasoning_content":"No default margins. The 4px offset is strange. It might be due to the Card having some internal flex layout or the h1 margin affecting layout. But it's only 4px in a 1280px viewport (0.3%). I think this is acceptable as \"centered\" within measurement tolerance.\n\nHowever, the Page height issue is a real concern. Page height is 169px, not filling the viewport. The design says \"The page content area fills the available viewport height between the header and footer.\" The current implementation uses an extra div wrapper with flex:1 to achieve vertical centering, but the Page component itself doesn't fill the height. This is a deviation from the design.\n\nBut wait - does the design actually require Page to fill the height, or just the \"page content area\"? The page content area in the implementation is the outer div, which does fill the height. The Page component is inside it. So semantically, the \"page content area\" (the area between header and footer) does fill the available height. It's just that the Page component isn't the one doing it.\n\nLooking at the source code, I think the intent of the design is that Page should handle the layout (fill height and center content). The developer added a wrapper div with custom inline styles instead of relying on Page. This adds custom CSS beyond the design system, which DESIGN.md says should not be needed.\n\nSo I should report this as a minor deviation: the visual result is correct (card centered between header/footer) but the implementation uses custom inline CSS on a wrapper div rather than having Page fill the height as designed.\n\nActually, wait. Maybe Page IS intended to be used with a wrapper that fills the height? AppShell might not provide a flex layout automatically. But DESIGN.md says \"Inside the shell: Header at the top... Footer at the bottom... The main content area uses the Page component so the single page scrolls once and centres content on large screens.\" This suggests Page is the main content area, not wrapped in a div.\n\nLet me also consider: the task says \"Do not modify the application code; only verify and report.\" So I just need to report.\n\nNow, for the verification report, I think:\n- Most requirements pass.\n- Card is visually centered (4px offset is measurement tolerance).\n- Page does not fill available viewport height; instead a custom wrapper div does.\n\nLet me also check the package.json for optional peers. It doesn't list mermaid, puppeteer, pixelmatch, pngjs. Good. It lists @codesweep-ai/ui. Good.\n\nLet me also check that the design system components are used rather than hand-styled elements. The data-component attributes all exist. The Button uses the design system. The Header/Footer use the design system. The h1 is custom but that's fine for the greeting text.\n\nWait - the task says \"Confirm the application uses `@codesweep-ai/ui` components: look for `data-component` attributes naming `Header`, `Footer`, `Button`, `Card`, `Page`, and `AppShell` in the rendered page.\" We confirmed all exist.\n\nNow I need to:\n1. Write a summary report to ~/.local/share/cs-campaign/output\n2. Commit work to own branch (the verification script, output, maybe a report)\n3. Run cs-campaign-member reply --file \u003csummary\u003e\n\nBut wait - I'm on detached HEAD at refs/campaign/orchestrator. I need to switch back to my branch and commit the report/output. The verification work (scripts in /tmp) don't need to be committed. But the output should be in ~/.local/share/cs-campaign/output, and I might commit a report to the hello-app repo.\n\nActually, the orientation says \"Commit your work to your own branch. Your machine is destroyed at the end of the campaign; a commit on your branch is the only way anything survives. Your reply automatically records whether your branch really differs from its base.\"\n\nWhat work should I commit? Probably a verification report in the repo. The output directory is also for durable results. I should create a report in the output directory and possibly commit it to the branch.\n\nThe orchestrator ref contains the app code; I should not commit that to my branch. I should switch back to my branch, add a report file, and commit it.\n\nLet me first stop the dev server, then switch back to my branch.\n\nActually I need the dev server to be running for the verification, but I already did the verification. I can stop it.\n\nLet me write the output report now."},"finish_reason":null,"index":0,"raw_output":null}],"created":1789959766,"id":"chatcmpl-6709a071b74a440c9d3cd1df2723a6f7","model":"accounts/fireworks/models/kimi-k2p7-code","object":"chat.completion.chunk","usage":null}

